# gRPC in Microservices – Interview Notes

---

## 📌 What is gRPC?

gRPC (Google Remote Procedure Call) is a high-performance communication protocol used for communication between microservices.

* Developed by Google
* Uses HTTP/2
* Uses Protocol Buffers (Protobuf) instead of JSON
* Faster than REST APIs

---

## 📌 Why use gRPC?

* High performance (binary data)
* Low latency
* Strongly typed contracts
* Supports streaming
* Ideal for microservices communication

---

## 📌 REST vs gRPC

| Feature     | REST               | gRPC                     |
| ----------- | ------------------ | ------------------------ |
| Protocol    | HTTP/1.1           | HTTP/2                   |
| Data Format | JSON               | Protobuf (binary)        |
| Performance | Slower             | Faster                   |
| Contract    | No strict contract | Strong contract (.proto) |
| Streaming   | Limited            | Built-in                 |

---

## 📌 How gRPC Works

1. Define service in `.proto` file
2. Generate server & client code
3. Client calls method like local function
4. gRPC handles communication

---

## 📌 gRPC Architecture

Client → gRPC Client Stub → Network → gRPC Server → Service Implementation

---

## 📌 PROTO FILE (IMPORTANT)

Example: product.proto

```proto
syntax = "proto3";

option csharp_namespace = "Catalog.Grpc";

service ProductProtoService {
  rpc GetProduct (GetProductRequest) returns (ProductResponse);
}

message GetProductRequest {
  string id = 1;
}

message ProductResponse {
  string id = 1;
  string name = 2;
  double price = 3;
}
```

---

## 📌 SERVER IMPLEMENTATION (.NET)

```csharp
public class ProductService : ProductProtoService.ProductProtoServiceBase
{
    public override async Task<ProductResponse> GetProduct(
        GetProductRequest request, ServerCallContext context)
    {
        // Simulate DB call
        var product = new ProductResponse
        {
            Id = request.Id,
            Name = "Laptop",
            Price = 50000
        };

        return await Task.FromResult(product);
    }
}
```

---

## 📌 REGISTER gRPC IN Program.cs

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddGrpc();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGrpcService<ProductService>();

app.MapGet("/", () => "gRPC server running");

app.Run();
```

---

## 📌 CLIENT IMPLEMENTATION (.NET)

```csharp
var channel = GrpcChannel.ForAddress("https://localhost:5001");

var client = new ProductProtoService.ProductProtoServiceClient(channel);

var request = new GetProductRequest { Id = "1" };

var response = await client.GetProductAsync(request);

Console.WriteLine(response.Name);
```

---

## 📌 TYPES OF gRPC COMMUNICATION

1. Unary (Request → Response) ✅ most common
2. Server Streaming (1 request → multiple responses)
3. Client Streaming (multiple requests → 1 response)
4. Bidirectional Streaming (both sides stream)

---

## 📌 WHEN TO USE gRPC?

* Microservices internal communication
* High performance systems
* Real-time streaming
* Backend-to-backend communication

---

## 📌 WHEN NOT TO USE gRPC?

* Browser-based frontend (limited support)
* Public APIs (REST preferred)
* Simple CRUD apps

---

## 📌 ADVANTAGES

* Fast and efficient
* Strong typing
* Code generation
* Built-in streaming
* Less bandwidth usage

---

## 📌 DISADVANTAGES

* Harder to debug (binary format)
* Not human-readable (unlike JSON)
* Limited browser support
* Learning curve

---

## 📌 gRPC in Your Project (IMPORTANT)

Example:

* Basket Service → calls Discount Service using gRPC

Flow:
Basket API → gRPC Client → Discount gRPC Server → Response

---

## 📌 Interview One-Liner

"gRPC is a high-performance RPC framework that uses HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers for fast and efficient communication between microservices."

